        rolemaps-to="P"
       >
      <?MarkedContent page="1" ?>In the early nineties, 
      <Link xmlns="http://iso.org/pdf2/ssn"
        >
       <?ReferencedObject type="Annot" page="1" ?>
      </Link>
      <?MarkedContent page="1" ?>GSM was deployed in many European countries. 
      <Link xmlns="http://iso.org/pdf2/ssn"
        >
       <?ReferencedObject type="Annot" page="1" ?>
      </Link>
      <?MarkedContent page="1" ?>GSM of
      <?MarkedContent page="1" ?>fered for the first time international roaming for mobile subscribers. The 
      <Link xmlns="http://iso.org/pdf2/ssn"
        >
       <?ReferencedObject type="Annot" page="1" ?>
      </Link>
      <?MarkedContent page="1" ?>GSM’s use of Time Division Multiple Access (
      <Link xmlns="http://iso.org/pdf2/ssn"
        >
       <?ReferencedObject type="Annot" page="1" ?>
      </Link>
      <?MarkedContent page="1" ?>TDMA) as its communication standard was debated at length. And every now and then there are big discussion whether Code Division Multiple Access (
      <Link xmlns="http://iso.org/pdf2/ssn"
        >
       <?ReferencedObject type="Annot" page="1" ?>
      </Link>
      <?MarkedContent page="1" ?>CDMA) should have been chosen over 
      <Link xmlns="http://iso.org/pdf2/ssn"
        >
       <?ReferencedObject type="Annot" page="1" ?>
      </Link>
      <?MarkedContent page="1" ?>TDMA.
     </text-block>
    </semantic-para>
   </section>
